            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Scene: A Creative That Ran for 3 Days Cost Me $200</h2>
<p>Last Wednesday, Xiao Li from a utility app noticed something strange: a video creative had a 42% video completion rate and a 3.8% CTR—nearly double the account average. Excited, he increased the budget.</p>
<p>The result? Over the next 48 hours, this "star creative" brought 120 clicks but zero registrations.</p>
<p>Xiao Li was confused: the metrics looked great. Why no conversions?</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Analysis: "Can Run" vs. "Can Sell" Are Different Things</h2>
<p>In Meta ad optimization, there's a classic trap—treating traffic metrics as conversion metrics.</p>
<p>Let's break down the issues with Xiao Li's creative:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Actual Data</th>
<th>Account Average</th>
<th>Verdict</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Video Completion Rate</td>
<td>42%</td>
<td>28%</td>
<td>✅ Pass</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Click-Through Rate</td>
<td>3.8%</td>
<td>1.9%</td>
<td>✅ Pass</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversion Rate</td>
<td>0%</td>
<td>6.2%</td>
<td>❌ Fail</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cost Per Acquisition</td>
<td>∞</td>
<td>$1.8</td>
<td>❌ Invalid</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Core Issue</strong>: This creative successfully sparked click impulses, but the landing page couldn't deliver on the promise. Users clicked in, found "goods not matching description," and bounced immediately.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judgment Criteria: Three Layers to Identify "Fake Hits"</h2>
<h3>Layer 1: Traffic Metrics (Can It Run?)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Video play rate &gt; 25%</li>
<li>CTR &gt; 1.5x account average</li>
<li>CPM below category average</li>
</ul>
<p>This layer is visible in Meta's backend and is where most advertisers fall into the "data illusion zone."</p>
<h3>Layer 2: Engagement Metrics (Are Users Interested?)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Page engagement rate</li>
<li>Add-to-cart rate (e-commerce) / registration completion rate (apps)</li>
<li>Purchase-related questions in comments</li>
</ul>
<p>This layer gets closer to "selling" ability but can still be inflated by discounts or gimmicks.</p>
<h3>Layer 3: Conversion Metrics (Can It Make Money?)</h3>
<ul>
<li>CPA vs. LTV ratio</li>
<li>7-day ROI</li>
<li>First-order repeat purchase rate</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Only when Layer 3 data is healthy is a creative truly "sellable."</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Steps: Creative Review SOP</h2>
<p>When you find creatives that "can run but can't sell," follow these steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pause and observe immediately</strong>: Don't shut it down right away, but don't add budget either. Run with a small budget for 7 days to get enough samples</li>
<li><strong>Check landing page alignment</strong>: Is there a gap between what the creative promises and what the landing page delivers?</li>
<li><strong>Split audience testing</strong>: The same creative may perform drastically differently across audience segments</li>
<li><strong>Compare with historical hits</strong>: Find common characteristics of "sellable" creatives—usually practical, authentic, and scenario-based content</li>
</ol>
<p>Back to Xiao Li's case: He shut down the creative on day 4 and discovered the landing page took over 5 seconds to load, with no core function entry visible above the fold. Users were tricked in by the creative but couldn't find "how to use it," so they gave up.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results: ROI from Negative to Positive After Adjustment</h2>
<p>Xiao Li did two things:</p>
<ul>
<li>Switched the landing page to show a "3-second quick start" screenshot on the first screen</li>
<li>Changed the creative's first 3 seconds to feature the tool interface with subtitle explanations</li>
</ul>
<p>The revised creative's completion rate dropped to 35% and CTR to 2.9%, but conversion rate jumped to 4.8% with CPA down to $1.2.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion</strong>: The purpose of creative isn't to "get clicks"—it's to "get users to complete the target action." Running is the ticket; selling is the destination.</p>
<hr />
<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Next time you review creatives, don't just look at play rate and CTR—first ask yourself: Have the users this creative brought in become customers you actually want?</p>
